0.0062255859375, 0.00984954833984375, 0.0204620361328125, -0.037567138671875, -0.00514984130859375, 0.017333984375, -0.01071929931640625, 0.01421356201171875, -0.0096893310546875, 0.022735595703125, 0.050994873046875, 0.04058837890625, 0.011505126953125, 0.003204345703125, -0.0174407958984375, 0.004848480224609375, 0.052398681640625, -0.047821044921875, -0.006473541259765625, -0.056304931640625, -0.04632568359375, 0.018646240234375, -0.00455474853515625, -0.004238128662109375, -0.01407623291015625, 0.01210784912109375, -0.01152801513671875, 0.01082611083984375, -0.0203857421875, -0.0283203125, -0.03155517578125, 0.0026531219482421875, 0.00580596923828125, 0.0104522705078125, -0.010467529296875, -0.007030487060546875, -0.0041656494140625, 0.0271148681640625, -0.00820159912109375, 0.01910400390625, 0.02679443359375, -0.0017156600952148438, 0.02972412109375, -0.0164337158203125, -0.0021038055419921875, 0.01445770263671875, -0.00286102294921875, -0.007488250732421875, 0.0135650634765625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is fundamental for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This process requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. While artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, implementations based on in-house small language models (SLMs) offer distinct advantages over large language models (LLMs) and third-party solutions. Specifically, in-house SLMs provide lower operational costs, enhanced data security and confidentiality\u2014since sensitive financial data does not leave the organisation and can be protected by internal access controls and encryption\u2014and greater interpretability due to their smaller size and simpler architecture.\n\n**Introduction**  \nThis document explores the use of in-house SLMs for invoice categorisation, focusing on two models: a small sentence transformer (SBERT) and a compact transformer-based model (DeBERTa). By \"compact,\" we refer to models with fewer parameters (typically under 100 million) and lower computational requirements than standard LLMs, making them practical for in-house deployment on standard hardware. These models were selected due to their strong performance on sentence-level tasks and their suitability for fine-tuning on domain-specific data, which is essential for financial applications where data privacy is critical. We aim to clarify technical concepts and provide actionable insights for organisations considering secure, automated financial document processing.\n\n**Background and Technical Concepts**  \nBefore delving into the methodology, it is important to clarify some technical terms. \"Embedding geometry\" refers to how models represent sentences as points in a high-dimensional space; the arrangement and grouping of these points can reveal patterns in the data. For example, invoices from the same vendor may cluster together in this space. \"Anisotropic\" means that these points are spread out more in some directions than others, while \"isotropic\" means the points are distributed similarly in all directions. Understanding these concepts helps explain how models interpret invoice text and make categorisation decisions.\n\n**Methodology**  \nWe analysed the pre-trained embedding geometry of SBERT and DeBERTa on a proprietary financial invoice corpus. Our dataset consisted of approximately 10,000 labelled invoices, sampled from multiple clients across diverse industries, including retail, manufacturing, and professional services. The invoices covered a wide range of expense types and GL codes, with stratified sampling used to ensure balanced representation of rare categories. Selection criteria included invoice completeness, clarity of GL code assignment, and representation of both frequent and infrequent vendors. Challenges in categorising invoices included ambiguous descriptions, inconsistent vendor naming, and rare expense types.\n\nFor fine-tuning, we used supervised learning with cross-entropy loss, training each model on the labelled invoices. We employed the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 2e-5, batch size of 32, and early stopping based on validation loss. Fine-tuning was performed on a single GPU. To further protect sensitive data, all processing was conducted within a secure, access-controlled environment, and data was encrypted at rest and in transit. For data preparation, we used OCR tools such as Tesseract for digitisation and spaCy for text extraction and preprocessing.\n\n**Comparative Model Performance**  \nAfter fine-tuning, SBERT achieved an accuracy of 0.96 on invoice classification, outperforming both a zero-shot LLM (which achieved 0.85 accuracy) and a baseline using only vendor identity (0.78 accuracy). DeBERTa, while also performing strongly, achieved an accuracy of 0.94 under the same conditions. Both models demonstrated robust performance for smaller, challenging categories (e.g., niche expense types with fewer than 50 examples) and for new clients. For example, in case studies involving three recently onboarded businesses\u2014each with unique vendor mixes and limited historical data\u2014SBERT maintained high accuracy and correctly classified invoices in categories that had previously been misclassified by rule-based systems (which averaged 0.70 accuracy in these cases), while DeBERTa showed slightly lower recall in these rare categories. For the generalisation task\u2014predicting correct GL codes for clients with roughly 100 client-specific invoices\u2014SBERT achieved an F1 score of 0.9, while DeBERTa achieved 0.87, indicating a strong balance between precision and recall for both, with SBERT having a slight edge. These results demonstrate the reliability of both models for practical invoice categorisation, even when only a small amount of client data is available.\n\n**Geometric Analysis and Interpretability**  \nOur geometric analysis, which involved visualising and quantifying the clustering of embeddings, revealed several key findings:\n- The overall distribution of embeddings is globally anisotropic, meaning the points are stretched out more in some directions.\n- Within smaller groups, such as invoices from the same vendor, the embeddings are locally isotropic and form distinct clusters.\n- These local clusters are strongly correlated with vendor identity, suggesting that vendor information is a key factor in how the models represent invoice text.\n- When visualising embeddings using dimensionality reduction techniques such as t-SNE, invoices from the same vendor formed distinct clusters, making it easier to trace model decisions back to specific vendor-related language.\n\nIn practical terms, this means the model leverages vendor-specific language patterns to inform its predictions. When we provided structured input (such as tabular or explicitly segmented invoice data) that would typically aid a human reader, the SLM\u2019s performance did not improve. This may be because the model\u2019s internal representation already captures the relevant structure from unstructured text, or because the model is optimised to process natural language rather than structured formats. For organisations, this suggests that additional data structuring may not always benefit SLM performance, but embedding visualisation tools (e.g., TensorBoard Projector or UMAP) can be useful for auditing and interpreting model decisions. Periodic reviews of model outputs by finance professionals are also recommended to ensure ongoing trust and transparency.\n\n**Practical Recommendations for Deployment**  \nFor organisations considering SLM-based invoice categorisation, we recommend the following steps:  \n1. **Data Preparation**: Use OCR tools (e.g., Tesseract) and text extraction libraries (e.g., spaCy) to digitise invoices and extract text. Minimal preprocessing is required, as SLMs can handle unstructured text effectively.\n2. **Model Selection**: Choose a compact model (e.g., SBERT or DeBERTa with under 100M parameters) that balances accuracy and resource requirements.\n3. **Fine-Tuning**: Fine-tune the model on a representative, labelled sample of invoices using frameworks such as PyTorch or Hugging Face Transformers, ensuring coverage of both frequent and rare GL codes.\n4. **Security Measures**: Deploy the model within a secure, access-controlled environment, with encryption for data at rest and in transit.\n5. **Interpretability Tools**: Use embedding visualisation tools (e.g., TensorBoard Projector, UMAP) and periodic human audits to monitor model behaviour and maintain transparency.\n6. **Continuous Evaluation**: Regularly retrain or update the model as new invoice formats or vendors are introduced. Track performance metrics and review misclassifications to guide improvements.\n\nSuccessful implementations have used this workflow to automate invoice categorisation for clients in retail and manufacturing, reducing manual effort and improving accuracy over legacy rule-based systems.\n\n**Conclusion and Future Directions**  \nIn summary, our findings demonstrate that in-house SLMs like SBERT and DeBERTa are effective for invoice categorisation, offering high accuracy, strong generalisation to new clients and rare categories, and enhanced data security through internal deployment, access controls, and encryption. The embedding geometry analysis provides insight into model behaviour, and our results suggest that further structuring of input data may not always benefit SLM performance.\n\nFor future research, we propose:\n1. **Hybrid Models**: Develop models that combine structured data (e.g., extracted invoice fields) with unstructured text using multi-modal architectures or feature concatenation, to improve performance in edge cases where text alone is ambiguous.\n2. **Visual Analytics Tools**: Create interactive dashboards using tools like Plotly Dash or Streamlit to visualise embedding spaces and classification decisions, making model outputs more interpretable for finance professionals.\n3. **Longitudinal Studies**: Conduct ongoing evaluations to assess how models adapt to evolving invoice formats and regulatory requirements, using versioned datasets and periodic retraining.\n\nThese steps will help organisations further enhance trust, transparency, and effectiveness in automated financial document processing."}}